{"qid":"Q521263","names":["Tibet"],"title":"Tibet","endonym":null,"capsule":"Tibet was a Buddhist theocratic state centered on the high plateau north of the Himalayas, governed from 1642 onward by the Dalai Lamas in Lhasa with the backing of Mongol and later Qing Chinese patrons. At its greatest autonomous extent in the 17th century, Tibetan religious authority extended across much of the plateau and into neighboring regions, though the Qing court formalized suzerainty after 1720 following the Dzungar invasion. Formal Qing administrative control tightened after 1793, and Tibet operated as a largely autonomous buffer state under nominal Chinese overlordship through the 19th century. British imperial pressure from India and the 1904 Younghusband expedition forced open diplomatic contacts; Qing collapse in 1912 allowed a de facto independence that persisted until the People's Liberation Army entered Lhasa in 1950 and the Seventeen Point Agreement of 1951 formally incorporated Tibet into the People's Republic of China.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Tibet's borders are poorly documented by precise surveys before the 20th century; the dataset geometry likely reflects approximate plateau extent derived from administrative gazetteers and treaty maps, with significant uncertainty in the northeast and northwest margins.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Tibet","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Tibet"},{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Tibet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tibet"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"History of Tibet","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ta